    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chinese estimates and Japanese claims of losses differ widely: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s official report of 5 November gave 187,200 killed and wounded, Japanese estimates were roughly 250,000, and the Japanese gave their own as 9,115 killed and 31,257 injured.</li><li>The Japanese expected to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> within days, but the Chinese held out for over three months, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> lost much of his best-trained army, including some 10,000 of the 25,000 junior officers trained at Whampoa between 1929 and 1937.</li><li>Historian Peter Harmsen holds that the battle presaged urban combat as it was to be waged throughout the rest of the twentieth century; it was the largest urban battle before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A Chinese air attack on the Japanese flagship Izumo on 14 August 1937 went wrong, and bombs fell on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> International Settlement; the day became known as Black Saturday.</p></aside>
    </section>

