    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">August 1937</span><span class="rf-text">Two Japanese naval personnel and a Chinese guard are found dead at Hongqiao Airport, and the Japanese begin sending reinforcements to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1937</span><span class="rf-text">Fighting begins between Chinese and Japanese forces in Zhabei, Wusong and Jiangwan, and on 14 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> declares its resistance to Japanese aggression</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1937</span><span class="rf-text">The 36th Division's attack towards the Huangpu docks pushes the Japanese back but cannot break through</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1937</span><span class="rf-text">The Japanese 3rd and 11th Divisions land on the northeast coast, lengthening the front</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1937</span><span class="rf-text">Baoshan falls on 6 September, and the Chinese hold Luodian for weeks at heavy cost</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1937</span><span class="rf-text">The fight for Wusong Creek and Dachang ends with Dachang's fall on 26 October, and the Chinese withdraw from the city's urban centre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1937</span><span class="rf-text">The Japanese 10th Army lands at Jinshanwei on 5 November, and on 8 November the Chinese order a general retreat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1937</span><span class="rf-text">The Wufu Line is penetrated on 19 November and the Xicheng Line given up on 26 November, and the fighting runs on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-shanghai" data-v="3d1148ff05" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The first Chinese attack is halted</b> The 88th Division attacked the Japanese positions in downtown Shanghai in the late afternoon. Direct fire from the entrenched Japanese and bombardment by the Third Fleet in the Huangpu halted it.</li><li><b>Operation Iron Fist</b> The 88th and 87th Divisions shelled the Japanese positions and then charged with infantry. Poor coordination between infantry and artillery and the shelling from the ships kept them from a major breakthrough, and by 18 August the attack was called off.</li><li><b>The 36th Division attacks towards the docks</b> The newly arrived 36th Division was sent in on 19 August, half of it towards the Hueishan docks on the northern side of the Huangpu, to cut the Japanese forces in two. It fought through the streets under fire from the buildings and from the Third Fleet.</li><li><b>Chinese tanks reach Broadway but the wharf holds</b> The Japanese were pushed back towards the docks, and by 21 August Chinese tanks had reached Broadway, the last street before the Huangpu. The Japanese held the high walls of the wharf and the Chinese could not finish the breakthrough.</li><li><b>Japanese troops land on the coast</b> The Japanese 3rd and 11th Divisions landed on the north-east coast under naval bombardment, and the Chinese brought troops from the city to meet them. The front line was lengthened from the city to the coast.</li><li><b>Baoshan falls</b> Chinese troops held the walled town of Baoshan against several Japanese assaults. The Japanese surrounded it, a tank assault with air support broke through the gate, and the town fell on 6 September.</li><li><b>The fight for Luodian</b> The Chinese concentrated around Luodian and the Japanese came inland against them with naval gunfire, tanks and aircraft. The Chinese were bled almost dry and gave up the town by the end of September.</li><li><b>Liujiahang and Gujiazhai fall</b> The Japanese 3rd Division attacked the Liujiahang and Gujiazhai positions on the right wing of the Chinese group army, while the 11th Division pursued it. After days of attacks and counterattacks the Chinese retreated.</li><li><b>The Japanese reach the Wusong Creek</b> The Chinese fell back to the south bank of the Wusong Creek, and the Japanese attacked across it. The line was breached after two days at one bridge, but the Japanese met further defences behind it.</li><li><b>The Guangxi counteroffensive</b> The Guangxi Army joined the line and counterattacked to retake the banks of the creek. It was poorly coordinated and bogged down, and the Japanese answered with artillery, tanks and poison gas.</li><li><b>Dachang falls and the Chinese leave the city</b> The Japanese pushed on to Dachang and took it. The Chinese then withdrew from downtown Shanghai, which they had held for almost three months, to a line behind the Suzhou Creek.</li><li><b>The Sihang Warehouse is held</b> A battalion of the 88th Division, some 411 men under Xie Jinyuan, made a stand in the Sihang Warehouse on the Suzhou Creek. Its thick walls and the nearness of the International Settlement nullified Japanese firepower, and several attacks were beaten back.</li><li><b>The Japanese cross the Suzhou Creek</b> On 30 October the Japanese crossed the Suzhou Creek, and the Chinese troops were in danger of encirclement. The Japanese 3rd Division began landing on the east side of the creek on 31 October.</li><li><b>The 10th Army lands at Jinshanwei</b> On 5 November the Japanese 10th Army, made up of units diverted from the Battle of Taiyuan, landed at Jinshanwei on Hangzhou Bay, a position held by only two companies of the 63rd Division.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo-bridge-incident"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-polo-bridge-incident.jpg" alt="Marco Polo Bridge Incident" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo-bridge-incident">Marco Polo Bridge Incident</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marco_Polo_Bridge_air_view.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sweeper tamonten, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since the Japanese invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931 and the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1932, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had been in armed conflict without an official declaration of war. These conflicts escalated in July 1937, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo-bridge-incident">Marco Polo Bridge Incident</a> triggered a full-scale war. Shanghai was China's largest and most cosmopolitan city, the world's fifth largest at the time and the country's main commercial hub and largest port.</p><p>By deliberately starting a major conflict at Shanghai, the Chinese leadership is believed to have aimed to divert the main Japanese effort to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> delta, stalling their north-to-south advance, to give time to move vital industries to the interior, and to bring sympathetic Western powers to China's side. There is no consensus on whether the strategy succeeded.</p><p>On 9 August Naval Sub-Lieutenant Isao Ōyama and his driver were found dead at the gate of Hongqiao Airport together with a guard of the Chinese Peace Preservation Corps. On 10 August the Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> General demanded that the Chinese withdraw the Peace Preservation Corps and dismantle their defence works, and the Japanese began sending in reinforcements. Chinese troops were also deployed to the area from 11 August. Talks failed: Japan demanded a Chinese withdrawal from Shanghai, and Mayor Yu would concede at most that Chinese troops would not fire unless fired upon.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek.jpg" alt="Chiang Kai-shek" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiang_Kai-shek%EF%BC%88%E8%94%A3%E4%B8%AD%E6%AD%A3%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Chinese side was the National Revolutionary Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhizhong">Zhang Zhizhong</a> commanded the 5th Army and the Nanjing-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> war zone at the start, and Chiang, with Chen Cheng and Gu Zhutong, later took over command duties. Its forces were equipped primarily with small-calibre weapons, against much greater Japanese air, naval and armour power, and the only heavy weapons it had against bunkers were 150 mm howitzers. The elite of its army were divisions trained and equipped by German advisers, among them Alexander von Falkenhausen.</p><p>The Japanese had the Special Naval Landing Forces in the city, bolstered by 1,400 marines on 18 August, and 6,300 by 19 August. On 15 August they formed the Shanghai Expeditionary Army from the 3rd and 11th Divisions under General Iwane Matsui, and later reinforced it with the 9th, 13th and 101st Divisions and other units. Their strongholds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongkou">Hongkou</a> salient were fortified with concrete, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbed-wire">barbed wire</a>, sandbags and machine guns, and the Third Fleet lay in the Huangpu.</p><p>Chiang's first plan was a surprise attack by the numerically superior Chinese to push the Japanese into the river, using Entscheidungsschlacht tactics recommended by Falkenhausen. Zhang estimated that it would take a week. By early October the Japanese had more than 200,000 troops in the region against over 700,000 Chinese, drawn from Chiang's own army and from units of many provincial and warlord armies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fighting began around 9 am on 13 August, when the Peace Preservation Corps exchanged fire with Japanese troops in Zhabei, Wusong and Jiangwan, and Japanese ships opened fire on Chinese positions. The Chinese offensive began on 14 August, but it was halted by the entrenched Japanese and by naval bombardment. A second assault, Iron Fist, began on 17 August and was called off by 18 August. The 36th Division then attacked towards the Huangpu docks from 19 August and by 21 August Chinese tanks reached Broadway, the last street before the river, but could not take the walled wharf.</p><p>On 23 August the Japanese 3rd and 11th Divisions landed on the northeast coast, some fifty kilometres from downtown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, and the front grew longer. The town of Baoshan fell on 6 September, and its defending battalion was killed apart from one soldier sent out with a message. The defence of Luodian, nicknamed the grinding mill of flesh and blood, cost the Chinese heavily, and by the end of September they gave it up. Fighting at Liujiahang and Gujiazhai followed.</p><p>In October the Japanese forced Wusong Creek and, after a Chinese counteroffensive failed on 21 October, took Dachang on 26 October. The Chinese left the city centre from that night, leaving a battalion to hold the Sihang Warehouse. The Japanese 10th Army landed at Jinshanwei on 5 November, and the Chinese ordered a general retreat on 8 November. The Wufu Line was penetrated on 19 November.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Shanghai&#8221; cites where it covers First attacks, Operation Iron Fist, Urban fighting, Air operations:</p><ul><li>Frederic E. Wakeman, Policing Shanghai, 1927–1937 (University of California Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s official report of 5 November gave 187,200 killed and wounded. Contemporary Japanese estimates were roughly 250,000 Chinese casualties including some 67,000 killed, Frank Dorn put total Chinese casualties at 240,000 and Hsi-Sheng Chi at some 300,000. The Japanese claimed 9,115 killed and 31,257 injured, a figure Harmsen and Richard Frank think understated. Benjamin Lai estimates 93,000 to 99,000 Japanese casualties and James Paulose 92,640.</p><p>Chinese elite units suffered some sixty percent disproportionate casualties, and Chiang lost some 10,000 of the 25,000 junior officers trained at Whampoa between 1929 and 1937. By the time the 88th Division defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> it was down to seven thousand men, three thousand of them new recruits. Chiang had to rely more on provincial commanders of questionable loyalty, and lost some of his political leverage over local warlords. Almost all the Chinese tanks were lost.</p><p>Atrocities were committed, especially by the Japanese. According to Matsui's diary, all 500 Chinese prisoners captured around Wusong were shot, and Japanese forces machine-gunned crowds of refugees by the Jessfield Railway Bridge and strafed refugee columns. Aircraft struck ambulances marked with the Red Cross. The Chinese used scorched earth tactics, burning buildings and fields and poisoning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>. On Black Saturday, 14 August, errant Chinese bombs killed between 700 and 950 civilians outright, with some 3,000 civilian dead and injured, mostly at the Great World entertainment centre.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Shanghai&#8221; cites where it covers Chinese retreat from Shanghai, Casualties:</p><ul><li>Benjamin Lai, Shanghai and Nanjing 1937: Massacre on the Yangtze (Osprey Publishing, 2017)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from official and unit records on both sides, from diaries and memoirs, and from eyewitnesses. Foreign journalists watched much of the fighting from the International Settlement; Edgar Snow compared it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>. Chinese records include the Ministry of Military Affairs' casualty counts and divisional reports, and Japanese ones include division statistics and battle summaries, Iwane Matsui's diary and the official records compiled by Ikuhiko Hata. Modern accounts rest on historians such as Peter Harmsen, Rana Mitter and Richard Frank.</p><p>The numbers are disputed. The Ministry of Military Affairs counted in 1938 some 229,382 Chinese casualties from 13 August to 15 November 1937, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> units had not reported, and put total losses at 300,000. A later ministry figure of 229,382 for the Third War Zone from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo-bridge-incident">Marco Polo Bridge Incident</a> to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> matches it exactly. Chinese wartime records give over 60,000 Japanese casualties, Jonathan Fenby some 70,000. Units on both sides claimed each other's losses and accused the other of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poison-gas">poison gas</a>; Japanese forces are confirmed to have used it at least thirteen times.</p><p>The origin of the fighting is also contested. Several accounts say Ōyama opened fire at the airport gate. Harmsen holds the shootout was staged to cover a killing by Chinese soldiers disguised as guards, and Shi Shuo, of the 9th Group Army, said a death row inmate was dressed in uniform and killed. Whether Ōyama was acting on higher orders is unknown.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was the first of twenty-two major engagements between the National Revolutionary Army and the Imperial Japanese Army, and is regarded by some historians as the first battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It destroyed much of the city. The Chinese resistance shocked the Japanese, who believed they could take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> within days and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> within months. China failed to bring about international intervention, though Chiang kept troops in the city to influence the Nine-Power Treaty Conference then meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>.</p><p>The Japanese advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> began on 11 November, and the fighting led at once into the Battle of Nanjing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> did not immediately occupy the International Settlement or the French Concession, moving into them after declaring war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and United Kingdom in 1941.</p><p>Harmsen wrote that it signalled the totality of modern urban warfare, and it was the largest urban battle before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> almost five years later. In 1940 the Chinese government named 14 August 1937 Air Force Day, after the 4th Flying Group under Gao Zhihang shot down six Japanese planes without loss. The defence of the Sihang Warehouse, watched by crowds across the creek, and the last stand at Baoshan became symbols of Chinese resistance.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>From 13 August to 26 November 1937 Chinese and Japanese forces fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> on land, in the air and at sea. The Japanese took the city, but the Chinese held out for over three months and lost much of their best troops, and casualty figures remain widely disputed.</em></p>

